ps://inspirehep.net/files/60b31eac2ea0b9ca11c2e3de7370ea20","label":"fig:tprof","source":"arxiv","caption":"Transverse light yield profiles for individual fibers (a), for the sums at each side and for the whole strip (b). The strip assembly is in the central position.","filename":"A1_tProf_SiPM.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"5afa947f1b98def438b6622eaa89cd7d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5afa947f1b98def438b6622eaa89cd7d","label":"fig:tprof","source":"arxiv","caption":"Transverse light yield profiles for individual fibers (a), for the sums at each side and for the whole strip (b). The strip assembly is in the central position.","filename":"A1_tProf_Strips.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"5b5d75bba052669b7b49880f01d434b9","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5b5d75bba052669b7b49880f01d434b9","label":"fig:tdiff","source":"arxiv","caption":"Time difference distribution (a) between averages from strip sides and its Gaussian fit with $\\sigma=1.1$~ns; dependence of this time difference (b) on the strip assembly position, the slope of the linear fit is 9.2~ns/m.","filename":"Tspread_A0_center_1.1ns.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"3500e56f0a0472c1c6c32faba87c5f73","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/3500e56f0a0472c1c6c32faba87c5f73","label":"fig:tdiff","source":"arxiv","caption":"Time difference distribution (a) between averages from strip sides and its Gaussian fit with $\\sigma=1.1$~ns; dependence of this time difference (b) on the strip assembly position, the slope of the linear fit is 9.2~ns/m.","filename":"Tdiff_A1_9.43nspm.png","material":"preprint"}],"license":[{"url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","license":"CC BY 4.0","material":"preprint"}],"texkeys":["DANSS:2021raa","Alekseev:2021egz"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2022-04-14"}],"keywords":[{"value":"Neutrino detectors","source":"author"},{"value":"Photon detectors for UV, visible and IR photons (solid-state) (PIN diodes, APDs, Si-PMTs, G-APDs, CCDs, EBCCDs, EMCCDs, CMOS imagers, etc)","source":"author"},{"value":"Scintillators, scintillation and light emission processes (solid, gas and liquid scintillators)","source":"author"},{"value":"Particle tracking detectors (Solid-state detectors)","source":"author"},{"value":"scintillators","source":"author"},{"value":"scintillation and light emission processes (solid, gas and liquid scintillators)","source":"author"},{"value":"neutrino: sterile","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"pi: irradiation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"energy resolution","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"photon: yield","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"upgrade: proposed","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"scintillation counter: plastics","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"background","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"nuclear reactor","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"microstrip","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"DANSS is a one cubic meter plastic scintillator detector  with a primary goal of sterile neutrino searches at a commercial  nuclear reactor. Due to its highly advantageous location, fine  segmentation and ability to change the distance to the neutrino  production origin, DANSS is ahead of many similar experiments around  the world in terms of the counting rate, signal to background ratio  and sterile neutrino exclusion regions. Yet a moderate energy  resolution of the detector prevents further progress in the physics  program. The main challenge of the planned upgrade is to achieve an  energy resolution of 12% at 1 MeV. The new design of the main  sensitive element — the plastic scintillation strip — is the  most important step forward. The strip prototypes were manufactured  and tested at the pion beam of the PNPI synchrocyclotron. More than  twice higher light output together with fairly flat detector  response uniformity, longitudinal timing information and other  optimizations will help to reach the upgrade goal. This paper  discusses the drawbacks of the current strip version, outlines the  new features of the proposed upgrade, describes the beam test  procedure and presents the test results reflecting the advantages of  the new strip design in comparison with the current version.","source":"IOP"},{"value":"DANSS is a one cubic meter plastic scintillator detector with a primary goal of the sterile neutrino searches at a commercial nuclear reactor. Due to its highly advantageous location, fine segmentation and ability to change the distance to the neutrino production origin DANSS is ahead of many similar experiments around the world in terms of the counting rate, signal to background ratio and sterile neutrino exclusion regions. Yet a moderate energy resolution of the detector prevents the further progress in the physics program. The main challenge of the planned upgrade is to achieve the energy resolution of 12% at 1 MeV. The new design of the main sensitive element - the plastic scintillation strip - is the most important step forward. The strip prototypes were manufactured and tested at the pion beam of the PNPI synchrocyclotron. More than twice higher light output together with fairly flat detector response uniformity, longitudinal timing information and other optimizations will surely help to reach the upgrade goal. The paper discusses the drawbacks of the current strip version, outlines the new features of the proposed upgrade, describes the beam test procedure and presents the test results reflecting the advantages of the new strip design in comparison with the currently used version.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1468455"},"raw_refs":[{"value":" 1  I. Alekseev, \"DANSS: Detector of the reactor AntiNeutrino based on Solid Scintillator\", JINST,11,P11011, DOI: 10.1088/1748-0221/11/11/P11011","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/1748-0221/11/11/P11011"],"urls":[{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.02896"}],"label":"1","title":{"title":"DANSS: Detector of the reactor AntiNeutrino based on Solid Sci